{
  "name": "macneal-harder-parallelogram-quad8",
  "reference": "Benchmark B2. MacNeal-Harder straight cantilever under in-plane tip shear: length 6, depth 0.2, thickness 0.1, E = 1e7, nu = 0.3, six elements along the length, root fully fixed, a total shear force of 1 on the tip edge. The reference tip deflection is 0.1081 (Euler-Bernoulli plus the Timoshenko shear term). Three meshes of the same six elements: regular rectangles, trapezoids whose interior nodes alternate +/-0.1 top and bottom, and parallelograms whose whole top row is shifted 0.2 (45 degree edges, so the specimen itself is a parallelogram and its own converged answer is 0.1061). This row is parallelogram quad8, tip deflection 0.0878413, 18.7 % from 0.1081, recorded against its own value rather than gated: this is the distortion sensitivity the benchmark exists to show. Refining each mesh to 4 x 4 elements per block recovers 0.10791, 0.10791 and 0.10428 respectively, so nothing here is a formulation defect, only distortion at six elements.",
